{:edb/db-before-t 1248 :edb/db-after-t 1249 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534561 50 #inst "2026-10-12T03:08:25.702Z" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186151817 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 2n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}, \\qquad\nB = \\left\\{  2^{-n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb {N}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A = \\{ 2, 4, 6,\\ldots \\}$\n\n- $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$\n\n- $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186151817 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 2n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}, \\qquad\nB = \\left\\{  2^{-n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb {N}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A = \\{ 2, 4, 6,\\ldots \\}$\n\nII. $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186151817 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{2n∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{2(1),2(2),2(3),…\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,4,6,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{{2}^{-n}∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{{2}^{-1},{2}^{-2},{2}^{-3},…\\} \\\\  & =\\{\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is \n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\{ 2, 4, 6, \\ldots \\} \\cap \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{8},\\ldots \\right\\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186151817 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={2n∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={2(1),2(2),2(3),…} \\\\  & ={2,4,6,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={{2}^{-n}∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={{2}^{-1},{2}^{-2},{2}^{-3},…} \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is \n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\{ 2, 4, 6, \\ldots \\} \\cap \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{8},\\ldots \\right\\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186151818 1074 "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\} \\cap \\{ v, w \\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{-1,1,\\sqrt{5}\\}⊔\\{v,w\\} & =\\{-1,1,\\sqrt{5}\\}\\cup \\{v,w\\} \\\\  & =\\{-1,1,\\sqrt{5},v,w\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186151818 1074 "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\} \\cap \\{ v, w \\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{-1,1,\\sqrt{5}}⊔{v,w} & ={-1,1,\\sqrt{5}}\\cup {v,w} \\\\  & ={-1,1,\\sqrt{5},v,w}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186151819 1074 "Notice that the sets are overlapping since\n\n$$\n\\{ 7,8 \\} \\cap \\{ 2,4,8 \\} = \\{ 8 \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nSo, for each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is tagged with the corresponding index, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}=\\{(7,1),(8,1)\\} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}=\\{(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & =\\{7,8\\}⊔\\{2,4,8\\} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & =\\{(7,1),(8,1)\\}\\cup \\{(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)\\} \\\\  & =\\{(7,1),(8,1),(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186151819 1074 "Notice that the sets are overlapping since\n\n$$\n\\{ 7,8 \\} \\cap \\{ 2,4,8 \\} = \\{ 8 \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nSo, for each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is tagged with the corresponding index, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}={(7,1),(8,1)} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}={(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & ={7,8}⊔{2,4,8} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & ={(7,1),(8,1)}\\cup {(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)} \\\\  & ={(7,1),(8,1),(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186152102 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425786 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186152102 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425787 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186152103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425785 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186152103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425788 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186152373 1050 420.0 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186153433 1064 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are **disjoint** if they have no element in common. In other words, their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 5,10 \\}\n$$\n\n \n\nThese sets are disjoint since their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\} \\cap \\{ 5,10 \\} = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $C=(-\\infty,5]$ and $D=(-2,\\infty)$ are *not* disjoint since the intersection of these sets is *not* empty:\n\n$$\nC\\cap D = (-\\infty,5] \\cap (-2,\\infty) = (-2,5] \\neq \\emptyset\n$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186153433 1064 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are **disjoint** if they have no element in common. In other words, their intersection is empty: $$A \\cap B = \\emptyset$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$A = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 5,10 \\}$$\n\nThese sets are disjoint since their intersection is empty:\n\n$$A\\cap B = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\} \\cap \\{ 5,10 \\} = \\emptyset$$\n\nOn the other hand, $C=(-\\infty,5]$ and $D=(-2,\\infty)$ are *not* disjoint since the intersection of these sets is *not* empty: $$C\\cap D = (-\\infty,5] \\cap (-2,\\infty) = (-2,5] \\neq \\emptyset$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186153434 1064 "The **disjoint union** of two sets is defined differently depending on whether the sets are non-overlapping (disjoint) or overlapping (have a non-empty intersection). We'll use the special symbol \"$\\sqcup$\" (square cup) to denote the disjoint union.\n\nThe disjoint union of two non-overlapping (disjoint) sets $A$ and $B$ is simply the union of the sets.\n\nFor example, since\n\n$$\\{ 0,2,4 \\} \\cap \\{ 1, 3 \\} = \\emptyset,$$\n\nwe find the disjoint union of these sets as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{0,2,4}⊔{1,3} & ={0,2,4}\\cup {1,3} \\\\ & ={0,1,2,3,4}\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186153434 1064 "The **disjoint union** of two sets is defined differently depending on whether the sets are non-overlapping (disjoint) or overlapping (have a non-empty intersection). We'll use the special symbol \"$\\sqcup$\" (square cup) to denote the disjoint union. \n\nThe disjoint union of two non-overlapping (disjoint) sets $A$ and $B$ is simply the union of the sets.\n\nFor example, since \n\n$$\n\\{ 0,2,4 \\} \\cap \\{ 1, 3 \\} = \\emptyset,\n$$\n\nwe find the disjoint union of these sets as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{0,2,4\\}⊔\\{1,3\\} & =\\{0,2,4\\}\\cup \\{1,3\\} \\\\  & =\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186153436 1064 "Now, consider the following overlapping sets:\n\n$$A_1 = \\{ \\square, \\triangle \\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{ \\heartsuit, \\square\\}.$$\n\nThese sets overlap because their intersection is non-empty. $$A_1 \\cap A_2 = \\{ \\square \\} \\neq \\emptyset.$$ To construct the disjoint union of these overlapping sets, we proceed as follows:\n\n- For each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is \"tagged\" with the corresponding index: $$\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} & & \\,\\to \\, & & {A}_{1}^{∗}={(◻,1),(△,1)} \\\\ & {A}_{2} & & \\,\\to \\, & & {A}_{2}^{∗}={(♡,2),(◻,2)}\\end{aligned}$$\n- As a result, the new sets $A_1^\\ast$ and $A_2^\\ast$ are non-overlapping. So, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets: $$\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & ={◻,△}⊔{♡,◻} \\\\ & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\ & ={(◻,1),(△,1)}\\cup {(♡,2),(◻,2)} \\\\ & ={(◻,1),(△,1),(♡,2),(◻,2)}\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186153436 1064 "Now, consider the following overlapping sets: \n\n$$\nA_1 = \\{ \\square, \\triangle \\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{ \\heartsuit, \\square\\}.\n$$\n\nThese sets overlap because their intersection is non-empty. \n\n$$\nA_1 \\cap A_2 = \\{ \\square \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTo construct the disjoint union of these overlapping sets, we proceed as follows:\n\n- For each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is \"tagged\" with the corresponding index:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}=\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}=\\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- As a result, the new sets $A_1^\\ast$ and $A_2^\\ast$ are non-overlapping. So, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & =\\{◻,△\\}⊔\\{♡,◻\\} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\}\\cup \\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1),(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186374261 1071 "Which of the following pairs of sets are disjoint?\n\n- ${3,4,5}$ and ${6,7,8}$\n- ${-4,-3,-2}$ and ${2,3,4}$\n- $[-3,0]$ and $[0,3]$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186374261 1071 "Which of the following pairs of sets are disjoint?\n\nI. $\\{3,4,5\\}$ and $\\{6,7,8\\}$\n\nII. $\\{-4,-3,-2\\}$ and $\\{2,3,4\\}$\n\nIII. $[-3,0]$ and $[0,3]$" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186374261 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Pair I is disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n\\{3,4,5\\}\\cap \\{6,7,8\\}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair II is also disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n\\{-4,-3,-2\\}\\cap \\{2,3,4\\}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair III is *not* disjoint since the intersection of the sets is not empty:\n\n$$\n[-3,0]\\cap [0,3]=\\{0\\}\\ne ∅\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186374261 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Pair I is disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n{3,4,5}\\cap {6,7,8}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair II is also disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n{-4,-3,-2}\\cap {2,3,4}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair III is *not* disjoint since the intersection of the sets is not empty:\n\n$$\n[-3,0]\\cap [0,3]={0}\\ne ∅\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186374289 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{5n∣n\\in Z\\},\\,B=\\{-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B=\\{-1,-2,-3,…\\}$\n\nII. $A\\cap B=∅$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186374289 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA={5n∣n\\in Z},\\,B={-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B={-1,-2,-3,…}$\n- $A\\cap B=∅$\n- $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186374289 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…\\} \\\\  & \\ne \\{-1,-2,-3,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{5n∣n\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\nA\\cap B=\\{…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…\\}\\cap \\{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…\\}=∅.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186374289 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…} \\\\  & \\ne {-1,-2,-3,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={5n∣n\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\nA\\cap B={…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…}\\cap {-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…}=∅.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534561 17592186045417 false] [17592186425761 1080 "∅" 13194139534561 17592186045417 true] [17592186425761 1